          "id": "4047365",
          "title": "Showing Clear Value",
          "duration": "14:17",
          "durationMinutes": 15,
          "description": "We want the website visitors to read your copy and\nShowing Clear Value\n\nWe want the website visitors to read your copy and get more and more excited as they scroll down the page. We do this by talking about benefits. But not just once. But over and over and over again. \n\nMarketers call this your value proposition. \n\nA great value proposition quickly communicates the specific benefits this product or service gives me.  \n\nWhen laying out the website, we want to display these benefits throughout the site visually. \n\nYou can use a value section anywhere on the website, and you can use it multiple times. You can never have too much value! \n\nWe often use them immediately after you solve the visitor's problem by telling them what your product or service is. \n\nOr immediately below the main header before you set up the problem. \n\nOr anywhere else you’d like! \n\nSo what’s in a value section?\n\nFirst, a value stack needs to be a shortlist of 3\n\nStudies show that a list of 3 is more easily remembered than even a list of one! A bunch of random ones will be forgotten, but a pattern of 3 has a cadence when read, like “stop, drop, and roll.”\n\nOf course, rules are made to be broken! Sometimes we stack 2 stacks and make a stack of 6.\n\nOr sometimes we make a stack of 4 or 5. \n\nHowever, as a rule of thumb, stick to 3.   \n\n\n\n\nSecond, it must be visually skimmable.\n\nThis means short statements\n\nWe often recommend having checkmarks to catch the eye visually \n\nOr an icon that represents that value \n\n\n\n\nThird, each of these statements needs to be consistent grammatically\n\nIf you lead we a verb, they all should lead with a verb\n\nIf you lead with an adjective, they all should lead with an adjective\n\nBasically, don’t change the format of each one. \n\n\n\n\nLast, build value if possible.\n\nThe first one communicates immediate value to the customer\n\nThe second one communicates short-term value \n\nAnd the third one communicates long-term value \n\nHere are a few examples to look at:\n\nhttps://willhouserva.com/commercial/\n\nhttps://willhouserva.com/residential/\n\nhttps://www.steelefinancial.org/home\n\nhttps://switchyourstance.com/\n\n\n\n\nSo let’s write value statements… \n\nWe want to dive into our \n\nEssential Questions Worksheet for our Solar Company\n\n and \n\nEssential Questions Demo Worksheet for our CPA Firm\n\nWrite down our list of as many benefit statements as possible.\n\nFor our solar company: \n\nReduction in electric bills/energy costs \n\nLowering increased utility costs\n\nA more energy-efficient home \n\nLower their carbon footprint/help the environment.\n\nIncreasing the value of their home\n\nThey feel proud and responsible for doing this.\n\nThey make back their money in 5 years. \n\nWe are looking for statements that might not have already been stated in previous website sections—or looking to restate something in a different way. \n\nI see:\n\nIncreasing the value of their home\n\nThey feel proud and responsible for doing this.\n\nLower their carbon footprint\n\nThey make back their money in 5 years. \n\nNow we need to write these so they flow and look nice. I like leading with verbs because they communicate action… \n\nSo you could do:\n\nIncrease the value of your home\n\n\n\n\n\nI want to write something about feeling proud or being responsible, but it almost feels like we are a parent right here… “A responsible homeowner does this…” So I’m not going to use this in this value stack. \n\nSo let’s use the other two.\n\nLower your carbon footprint \n\nMake back your money in 5 years\n\nThese 3 are great. \n\nAnd they mostly follow the “immediate, short term, and long term benefit” progression. \n\nLet’s look at our CPA. \n\nSave time\n\nRelieve stress\n\nPay less taxes\n\nAvoid penalties\n\nGetting organized\n\nDoing right \n\nSaving money\n\nBooks in order/organized\n\nMinimize tax liability\n\nMaximize cash flow \n\nPredictable finances\n\nPeace of mind\n\nConfidence\n\nPull from the list of things that have not yet been stated on the site.\n\nMaybe we hit up the use of adjectives to describe these… \n\nMore time\n\nLess stress\n\nPredictable finances \n\nI love the simplicity of this! \n\nAnd because we have so much benefit language with our CPA we can create a 2nd or 3rd value stack and put it somewhere else on the homepage or on the website! \n\nSo remember, stack value over and over in the website to make visitors more excited for what you are selling!\n\nHappy copywriting!",
